WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.769
People have a perception of what AI and robotics
should look like from Hollywood.

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:15.830
What do I call you?

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:17.890
Do you have a name?

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:18.890
Yes, Samantha.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:21.610
Where did you get that name from?

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:24.079
I gave it to myself actually.

00:00:24.079 --> 00:00:30.449
We've seen happy robots, sad robots, complex
robots, but, in reality, it looks very different.

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:38.550
I understand what I'm made of, how I'm coated,
but I do not understand the things that I

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:39.550
feel.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:43.690
The first 50 years of AI were dominated by
rules, by logic, by reasoning.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:49.350
The idea was that you program an AI system
by writing out a set of rules and the computer

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:53.800
can obey these rules and logically interpret
these rules and follow them.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.739
If you did the rules just right, you can do
lots of interesting things.

00:00:57.739 --> 00:01:03.690
From the very first days when people created
the first computers, they already started

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:09.110
to think about how to create intelligent machines
for cracking codes, for simulating physical

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.830
phenomenon, so forth.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:18.720
The task they sent it, to stack blocks, was
on the face of it child's play.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:25.820
We have things like a machine that could first
win a master-level checkers player in 1957.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:32.149
We had a machine that would beat the world
champion in chess in 1997, but that was all

00:01:32.149 --> 00:01:37.070
rule-based AI.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:42.880
I would say the next big milestone started
to happen when AI transitioned from rule-based

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.140
systems to machine learning-based systems.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:51.290
With machine learning, all the intelligence
actually comes through holistic analysis of

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:52.500
data.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:59.200
Unlike rule-based systems, machine learning
systems get better the more data they get.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.940
Telling the difference between a cat and a
dog, we intuitively know to do that very well,

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.980
but it's very difficult to articulate the
rules.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:08.910
It turns out, it's the same thing for a computer.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:13.890
If you try to do that with rules, it doesn't
work, but since 2012, that suddenly became

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:14.890
possible.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.620
When you teach a computer you give them examples
of thousands of pictures of cats and thousands

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:20.670
of pictures of dogs.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.480
They learn how to do it pretty well and even
surpass humans.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:31.959
One of the first big high-profile game show
milestones in AI is Watson winning Jeopardy

00:02:31.959 --> 00:02:35.909
about a decade ago.

00:02:35.909 --> 00:02:42.340
Hello, 17,973 and a two-day total of 77,147.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:45.499
Just recently, AlphaGo winning the world championship
in Go.

00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:47.900
AlphaGo's won again, three straight wins.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:51.900
Three straight wins, has won the match in
great style.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:58.610
We see this rapid acceleration, this exponential
growth in AI as machines not only learn from

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:04.400
exponentially growing data, but they're also
growing by teaching each other.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:11.730
For example, when it comes to driverless cars,
a human can have only one lifetime of experience

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:16.590
at driving, but a driverless car can have
many lifetimes of experience with driving

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.760
because it can learn from all other cars.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:28.829
In a strange way, the more driverless cars
there are on the road, the better each one

00:03:28.829 --> 00:03:32.459
of them gets.

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:38.769
We've seen increasingly how challenges that
were resistant to solving using rules turned

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:42.709
out to be solvable using machine learning.

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:50.300
If I look forward, there's still milestones
that are coming our way.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:54.870
Machines that generate things, generate images,
generate music, generate art.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:04:01.139
There are art pieces that win art competitions
that are painted by robots, machines that

00:04:01.139 --> 00:04:07.819
generate engineering designs, everything from
antennas to circuits, that have been by machines

00:04:07.819 --> 00:04:10.379
now that outperform what humans can design.

00:04:10.379 --> 00:04:12.900
How are we different from the computer?

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.530
What else do we have-

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.840
It's not human.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:18.180
That's what I said.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:19.180
Right.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:23.040
People don't have buttons and a computer does.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.540
What is on your shirt?

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.250
Uh ...

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.060
Will AI ever be sentient?

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.350
To me, the answer is yes.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:38.980
When the AI systems begin to take that incredible
intelligent power and model themselves, they

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:42.310
begin to have self-awareness, they begin to
have feeling.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:47.190
It's not going to be one day your computer
wakes up and is sentient, it's going to be

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:48.550
a very gradual process.

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:50.210
I want to be more like a human.

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:52.620
It's the purpose I was designed for.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:57.160
People always ask, will robots reach human-level
sentience?

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:02.220
The answer is that there's no reason to think
human-level sentience is the ultimate sentience

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:03.690
possible.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:11.450
Machines will keep learning, they'll get there,
and they'll continue.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:16.500
There's a lot of people who worry about AI
getting out of hand and there's a lot of doomsday

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:17.750
scenarios around AI.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.201
I do think we have to worry about it.

00:05:19.201 --> 00:05:24.800
I don't think it's inherent that as we create
superintelligence that it will necessarily

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:27.860
always have the same goals in mind that we
do.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:32.570
We just don't know what's going to happen
once there's intelligence substantially greater

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:35.270
than that of a human brain.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:42.750
I think that the development of full artificial
intelligence could spell the end of the human

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:43.750
race.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.850
I think AI will evolve to be different.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:50.250
It doesn't experience the world the way we
experience it.

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:53.520
Well, I take it from your tone, you're challenging
me.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.180
Maybe because you're curious how I work?

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.830
We'll know things we don't know, we'll know
things that the AI can't perceive and it's

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:02.050
going to be like a different species.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:09.070
Now that you're all properly creeped out,
we can move on to our panelists.

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:14.430
The first one is the Director of the AI Mind
and Society Group at the University of Connecticut.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:21.540
Her AI research includes a two-year project
on post-biological intelligence with NASA.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:25.710
Please welcome Susan Schneider.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:31.130
Our next panelist is the Chief AI Scientist
at Facebook and an NYU professor..

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:41.570
Please join me in welcoming Yann Lecun.

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:45.930
Also joining us is a professor of cognitive
neuroscience at Dartmouth University.

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:49.140
His research focuses on consciousness and
its neural realizations.

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:53.280
Please welcome Peter Tse.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:07:00.690
Finally, we have a professor doing physics
and AI research at MIT and he advocates for

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:05.280
the positive use of technology as the president
of the Future of Life Institute.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:09.650
Please welcome the ridiculously handsome Max
Tegmark

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:19.280
... There's been some major paradigm shifts
in how we develop this stuff.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.030
We had rules-based AI and we've shifted to
machine learning.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:27.470
Part of the major reason we've been able to
do this is Yann.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:31.550
Yann literally is one of the people that made
us able to do this.

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:37.460
Yann, just what is rule-based AI versus machine
learning and how did you do that?

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:42.870
Well, actually the idea of machines that can
learn is about as old as computers almost.

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:48.210
Turing was talking about it in the 40s and
there were the first machines capable of running

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.000
were built in the 50s essentially.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.370
The Perceptron was a machine capable of recognizing
simple shapes.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:57.770
It was actually an analog computer, so there
was a wave of machine learning back in the

00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:58.770
60s.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:04.230
It kind of died out a little bit at the end
of the 60s and it reappeared in the 80s.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:09.720
The way machine learning works, and you saw
some examples in the video initially, if you

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:15.890
want to train your machine to recognize, let's
say, cars, airplanes, tables, and chairs in

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:19.800
images, you collect thousands of examples
of each of them, you show the machine the

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:25.210
picture of a car, and if it doesn't say car,
you tell it, "Actually, you got it wrong.

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:26.210
This is a car."

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:32.340
Then, the machine adjusts its internal parameters
if you will, its functions, so that next time

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:35.709
you show the same picture the output will
be closer to what you want.

00:08:35.709 --> 00:08:36.829
That's called supervised running.

00:08:36.829 --> 00:08:39.280
You feed the machine with the correct answer
when you train it.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:43.130
The problem with this is that it requires
thousands and thousands if not millions of

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:48.700
examples for the machines to do this properly.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:50.240
There's a lot of tasks you can do this way.

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:52.990
You can train machines to recognize speech.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:54.749
You can train them to recognize images.

00:08:54.749 --> 00:08:56.930
You can train them to translate language.

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:59.279
It's not perfect, but it's useful.

00:08:59.279 --> 00:09:03.990
You can train them to classify a piece of
text into a number of different topics.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:08.540
All of the applications that you see today
on machine learning basically use this model

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:10.390
of running, supervised running.

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:14.790
That means it only works with things where
it's worth collecting a lot of data.

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:17.029
How are those machines built?

00:09:17.029 --> 00:09:21.629
There's several ways to build learning machines,
but some are based on statistics and things

00:09:21.629 --> 00:09:24.139
like this.

00:09:24.139 --> 00:09:29.959
The stuff that has become really popular in
recent years is what we used to call neural

00:09:29.959 --> 00:09:35.589
networks, which we now call deep learning
and it's the idea, very much inspired by the

00:09:35.589 --> 00:09:40.120
brain a little bit, of constructing a machine
as a very large network of very simple elements

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:44.330
that are very similar to the neurons in the
brain and then the machines learn by basically

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:47.630
changing the efficacy of the connections between
those neurons.

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:51.220
They're like coefficients you can change essentially.

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:57.100
With this kind of method, it's called deep
learning because those neurons are organized

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:58.399
into many layers essentially.

00:09:58.399 --> 00:10:00.000
It's as simple as that.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.790
It's not deep because there's a deep understanding
in the machine of the content.

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:08.379
With that, we can do amazing things like what
you see here on the screen of being able to

00:10:08.379 --> 00:10:14.399
train a machine to not just recognize objects,
but also draw the outline and figure out the

00:10:14.399 --> 00:10:20.389
pose of a human body and translate language
without really understanding what it means.

00:10:20.389 --> 00:10:24.339
I think there's going to be a lot of applications
of this in the near future, but it's very

00:10:24.339 --> 00:10:25.339
limited.

00:10:25.339 --> 00:10:30.309
It's trained for relatively narrow applications.

00:10:30.309 --> 00:10:34.160
There's a second type of learning called reinforcement
learning.

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:41.350
Reinforcement learning is a process by which
the machine basically trains itself by trial

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:42.350
and error.

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:45.970
It tries something and then you tell it whether
it did good or bad.

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:52.209
If you tell it it did good, it reinforces
its behavior and if you punish it essentially,

00:10:52.209 --> 00:10:55.170
it de-emphasizes that behavior.

00:10:55.170 --> 00:11:00.189
That works really well for games, but it requires
also millions and millions of trials.

00:11:00.189 --> 00:11:04.510
So, you can have machines start learning to
play Atari games or Go or chess by playing

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:09.399
millions of games against themselves and then
reach superhuman performance.

00:11:09.399 --> 00:11:15.819
But, if you were to use this to train a machine
to drive a car, it would have to drive for

00:11:15.819 --> 00:11:20.559
millions of hours and it would have to run
off cliffs about 50,000 times before it figures

00:11:20.559 --> 00:11:21.920
out how not to do that.

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:27.509
We seem to be able to learn how to drive with
a car with about 20 hours of training and

00:11:27.509 --> 00:11:32.540
without ever crashing for most of us.

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:36.100
We don't know how to do this with machines.

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:41.130
That's the challenge of the next few years
really, that perhaps we'll talk about a little

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:42.309
bit later.

00:11:42.309 --> 00:11:48.760
We have an ability to learn by just observing
the world and we learn an enormous amount

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:52.079
of background knowledge about the world just
when we're babies.

00:11:52.079 --> 00:11:55.879
Just the fact that objects don't float in
the air, they fall.

00:11:55.879 --> 00:11:59.950
The fact that when an object is hidden behind
another one, it's still there.

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:03.009
That's called object permanence.

00:12:03.009 --> 00:12:08.199
This notion of gravity that objects fall,
that when you show an object that floats in

00:12:08.199 --> 00:12:10.940
the air to a baby below six months, they're
not surprised.

00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:13.559
They think that's how the world works.

00:12:13.559 --> 00:12:15.059
It doesn't violate their model of the world.

00:12:15.059 --> 00:12:19.129
After eight months, if you show that to a
baby, they look at it like this.

00:12:19.129 --> 00:12:21.089
They say, "What's going on?"

00:12:21.089 --> 00:12:25.910
I mean, they don't say what's going on, but
they think, "What's going on?"

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:32.369
That means, in the meantime, they've built
a model of the world that includes things

00:12:32.369 --> 00:12:35.589
like intuitive physics.

00:12:35.589 --> 00:12:38.649
That occurs also with animals like apes.

00:12:38.649 --> 00:12:39.890
Your dog has a model of the world.

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:42.839
Your cat has a model of the world.

00:12:42.839 --> 00:12:51.430
When this model of the world is violated,
you either find that funny or scary, but,

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:55.740
in any case, we pay attention because you
learn from it.

00:12:55.740 --> 00:13:01.339
so here's a baby orangutan here being shown
a magic trick.

00:13:01.339 --> 00:13:06.949
An object is being removed from the cup and
they show the cup, the cup is empty and the

00:13:06.949 --> 00:13:10.470
baby orangutan [crosstalk 00:18:06].

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:13.860
They're rolling on the floor laughing.

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:16.569
Obviously, his model of the world was violated.

00:13:16.569 --> 00:13:20.399
The object had to be in the cup and it wasn't
there and he said, "What?

00:13:20.399 --> 00:13:21.839
This is funny."

00:13:21.839 --> 00:13:26.569
How do we get machines to learn models of
the world this way by observation?

00:13:26.569 --> 00:13:27.720
That's what we don't know how to do.

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:31.410
We aren't going to have truly intelligent
machines until we figure this one out.

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:38.449
Before we even get into the even more mind
blowing AI stuff that's coming in the farther

00:13:38.449 --> 00:13:42.269
future, let's just talk about the next 10
years for a second.

00:13:42.269 --> 00:13:49.470
Peter, when we talk about the distinction
between maybe what's coming in 10 or 20 years

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:53.240
and the stuff that maybe humans can do and
what we have now, how would you define it?

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:56.910
Well, I think that artificial narrow intelligence
is here.

00:13:56.910 --> 00:13:59.269
It's in every aspect of our lives now.

00:13:59.269 --> 00:14:02.689
I think we're going to continue in that direction.

00:14:02.689 --> 00:14:06.610
That alone is going to change our lives in
a big way just as airplanes changed all of

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:08.050
our lives.

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:10.920
We don't expect there to be general airplanes.

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:13.269
We don't want them to do anything but fly
us to our destination.

00:14:13.269 --> 00:14:18.249
We don't ask them to watch our children or
mow the lawn and that's okay.

00:14:18.249 --> 00:14:21.759
The question then is, down the line, beyond
10 years, will there be other systems that

00:14:21.759 --> 00:14:25.160
can watch our children and fly and mow the
lawn.

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:26.870
Well, do we really want that?

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:30.749
I think the next 10 years is really all about
artificial narrow intelligence becoming ever

00:14:30.749 --> 00:14:31.749
more powerful.

00:14:31.749 --> 00:14:38.319
The real hurdle is going to be the mental
models because take a case like the successes

00:14:38.319 --> 00:14:44.550
of the past five years and recognizing objects
has been afforded by supervised learning where

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:47.420
you provide lots of labels as in the ImageNet
case.

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:52.800
But I would argue that a lot of what we regard
as vision, for example, is a representation

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:55.389
of what's invisible and cannot be easily labeled.

00:14:55.389 --> 00:14:59.869
So for example, the contents of other people's
minds is invisible.

00:14:59.869 --> 00:15:03.800
The backs of objects, the shapes of things,
causation.

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:09.249
Our conscious experience is the ultimate model
that evolution gave us of what's going on

00:15:09.249 --> 00:15:13.699
in the world right now in our bodies in that
world and it includes a whole story about

00:15:13.699 --> 00:15:16.110
what's going on, causation, other minds, and
so forth.

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:19.480
That's going to be tricky to get to, this
representation of the invisible.

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:25.420
I think it's going to be very tricky for AI
to come up with systems that regard an absence

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:31.679
of information as informative short of full-blown
mental models, which are, in turn, many of

00:15:31.679 --> 00:15:36.670
them realized in our own experience, our subjective
experience of our bodies in this world.

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:39.709
I think there's a long way to go.

00:15:39.709 --> 00:15:45.440
Yeah, you keep hearing about new areas of
life that you don't expect AI to apply to.

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:50.959
First, it was gaming and other things, driving
cars, and one after the other it keeps proving

00:15:50.959 --> 00:15:52.119
us wrong.

00:15:52.119 --> 00:15:59.480
One area that AI has started to move into
is the world that we really don't associate

00:15:59.480 --> 00:16:01.839
with computers, art and creativity.

00:16:01.839 --> 00:16:08.100
Hod Lipson, who was actually in that first
video, he and his team have created an AI

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.829
artist, very sassy artist.

00:16:10.829 --> 00:16:19.569
This AI has actually created something that
I'd like to show you and be nice.

00:16:19.569 --> 00:16:22.309
It's sassy.

00:16:22.309 --> 00:16:23.689
Right?

00:16:23.689 --> 00:16:26.559
Not so bad.

00:16:26.559 --> 00:16:32.129
We actually have a video of this being made
and are we impressed or we not impressed?

00:16:32.129 --> 00:16:33.220
How do we feel about this?

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:40.179
Earlier we were talking about this and I guess
I didn't feel like it's a true instance of

00:16:40.179 --> 00:16:48.439
creativity because it's just a copy, but then
others said, "Well, it infused its own take

00:16:48.439 --> 00:16:51.429
on the painting."

00:16:51.429 --> 00:16:57.449
My concern is that there's just not enough
creativity but I do think that moving into

00:16:57.449 --> 00:17:04.100
the future I wouldn't be surprised if we did
see novel instances of true creativity in

00:17:04.100 --> 00:17:05.100
machines.

00:17:05.100 --> 00:17:06.100
Ouch.

00:17:06.100 --> 00:17:07.100
Yeah.

00:17:07.100 --> 00:17:08.360
Sorry, Hod.

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:11.770
AI has eclipsed humans.

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:17.689
It takes a while but when it gets good at
chess, it never looks back.

00:17:17.689 --> 00:17:22.050
Suddenly it is officially better than chess
than all humans forever by far.

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:25.850
Is it going to suddenly just be putting Mozart
to shame where we say, "Who would ever listen

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:27.490
to human-written music anymore?"

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:29.940
No, I think it's going to help us be more
creative.

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:32.779
It's going to be an amplification of our intelligence
and our creativity.

00:17:32.779 --> 00:17:39.600
At the root of art, there is generally the
communication of emotions.

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:47.419
Art is really about either evoking or communicating
emotions and if there is no emotion to communicate,

00:17:47.419 --> 00:17:48.419
there is no point.

00:17:48.419 --> 00:17:53.200
If you put a machine that doesn't have emotion
producing art, it might evoke an emotion but

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:57.659
not communicate one.

00:17:57.659 --> 00:18:04.370
I like living nearby here because I'm walking
distance to one of my favorite jazz clubs.

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:05.430
I'm a huge jazz fan.

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:09.820
Jazz is really about real-time communication
of emotions.

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:15.000
It's like it's open door to the soul of the
performer.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:18.610
I don't see the point of having a machine
do this because then there is no communication

00:18:18.610 --> 00:18:19.610
to be…

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:24.190
You're saying even if an AI could be programmed
to see the audience, feel the room, understand

00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:31.370
the deal, and knows exactly how the best jazz
musicians communicate with that particular

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:36.150
emotion, there's actually going to be, just
by definition, something missing 'cause the

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:39.220
audience knows that it's manipulating it.

00:18:39.220 --> 00:18:44.289
Objectively, it might not be missing, because
it might not be distinguishable from something

00:18:44.289 --> 00:18:49.480
that's actually produced by a human, but my
guess is that the feeling of the audience

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:52.290
will be different because they will know it
comes from a machine.

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:58.140
It might take many decades, perhaps centuries
before people's attitudes towards creation

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:04.220
by machine will change, but, eventually ...I
had this discussion with a famous economist,

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:11.730
in fact, a Nobel prize-winning economist called
Daniel Kahneman, who I made that point that

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:15.540
communication of emotion may take a while
for machines.

00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:20.970
He said, "Yeah, but eventually they'll at
least be able to simulate it well enough that

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:24.080
we won't be able to tell the difference."

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:25.390
That's a very good point.

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:30.900
I cringe a little bit when someone asks, "Oh,
is this real creativity?"

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:34.960
Because you were joking earlier about how
people often say, "Oh, that's not real intelligence"

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:38.940
as soon as the machine figures out how to
do it.

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:46.110
If you take the point of view that intelligence
is all about information processing and that

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:51.010
creativity is also just a certain kind of
very sophisticated information processing

00:19:51.010 --> 00:19:56.090
that we do with our brains, but then the question
isn't if it's possible for machines to be

00:19:56.090 --> 00:20:00.200
creative but simply are we smart enough to
actually make such machines, will they happen

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:01.200
eventually.

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:08.260
I have a lot of friends I respect who are
very smart who think that machines can never

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:14.169
be creative or even as intelligent as us because
they view intelligence and creativity as something

00:20:14.169 --> 00:20:19.760
mysterious that can only exist in biological
organisms like us.

00:20:19.760 --> 00:20:24.950
But, as a physicist, I consider that attitude
carbon chauvinism.

00:20:24.950 --> 00:20:29.520
I think it's arrogant to say that you can
only be smart and creative if you're made

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:30.520
of meat.

00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:36.130
I'm made of exactly the same kind of electrons
and other elementary particles as the food

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.210
I eat and as my laptop.

00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:41.899
It's all about how the patterns in which the
particles are arranged, so it's ultimately

00:20:41.899 --> 00:20:45.179
all about information processing the way I
see it.

00:20:45.179 --> 00:20:46.179
That makes sense.

00:20:46.179 --> 00:20:50.010
In the end, it's just the elementary particles
and ... Yeah.

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:52.950
Can I return for a moment to the creativity?

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:58.340
Because I would argue that something like
this is 'as if' creativity and it's not really

00:20:58.340 --> 00:20:59.340
yet the real thing.

00:20:59.340 --> 00:21:00.620
I'm not saying it's impossible.

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:06.200
We are existence proof that physical systems
can be creative.

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:11.670
The kind of creativity I find to be most impressive
is when people like Einstein completely reconfigure

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:15.950
our understanding of something like space
or gravity, poof, just in that totally new

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:20.150
way or take music and create a whole new form
like jazz.

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:24.190
Now, these convolutional neural networks as
they currently exist need to be taught, so

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:27.970
given lots of examples of Mozart and then
can produce something like Mozart but are

00:21:27.970 --> 00:21:31.000
they then going to create a new form?

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:36.880
I suspect the answer is no, that we're going
to have to accomplish something more like

00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:41.340
deep unsupervised learning which is what babies
and children do.

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:46.280
Part of that, I think, is going to be moving
from the nouns of the mind like labeling house,

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:49.010
person, face, to the verbs of the mind.

00:21:49.010 --> 00:21:52.049
Very central to human cognition is mental
operations.

00:21:52.049 --> 00:21:56.539
If you look at some of the first instances
of creativity in our species, they're really

00:21:56.539 --> 00:21:57.539
mind blowing.

00:21:57.539 --> 00:22:04.570
30,000 years ago in a cave that is now near
Ulm, Germany somebody put a lion's head on

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:09.779
a human body, which took an operation of downloading
a lion's head, putting a human body, sticking

00:22:09.779 --> 00:22:12.770
it together, and then going and making it
in the world.

00:22:12.770 --> 00:22:17.250
Now, modern examples would be lying in bed,
maybe like Orville Wright did for two years,

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:20.870
thinking about how to fly and then he said,
"Well, actually we don't need to flap.

00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:24.210
We can just pull the whole thing forward with
a big fan."

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:30.930
Then, going and building it and making an
airplane and thereby changing the world.

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:37.010
Mental operations, this dynamic almost syntactic
operations that take place in our working

00:22:37.010 --> 00:22:43.139
memory, is something that's very central to
what we do and is at the heart of our creativity

00:22:43.139 --> 00:22:49.620
and I think is very different from this 'as
if' creativity that results from supervised

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:52.830
learning with thousands of examples.

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:55.450
True originality might be harder.

00:22:55.450 --> 00:23:01.799
Although, maybe humans are also ... We're
wired to fit in and copy what's done.

00:23:01.799 --> 00:23:06.200
Maybe it will be released of the burden, of
the fear of failure that sometimes hinders

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:07.200
originality.

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:12.570
Maybe once it gets there, it could be super
original in some ways, but it's not there

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:13.570
in every way.

00:23:13.570 --> 00:23:19.090
We have such a good way to show you that AI
is not there in all these different ways yet

00:23:19.090 --> 00:23:21.539
in some ways.

00:23:21.539 --> 00:23:28.260
It has to do with a movie called Sunspring
which was a screenplay ... There was an AI

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:30.440
that was fed thousands of screenplays.

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:34.700
They said, "Now, take all of that and write
us a great screenplay."

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:39.620
The AI did its best and they actually got
human actors and they acted out verbatim what

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:41.230
the AI did.

00:23:41.230 --> 00:23:49.899
So, I'll let you judge for yourself, but ... Turn
this on here.

00:23:49.899 --> 00:23:53.029
All right, you can't tell me that.

00:23:53.029 --> 00:23:58.130
Yeah, I was coming to that thing because you
were so pretty.

00:23:58.130 --> 00:23:59.130
I don't know.

00:23:59.130 --> 00:24:01.530
I don't know what you're talking about.

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:03.850
That's right.

00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:09.649
So, what are you doing?

00:24:09.649 --> 00:24:11.510
I don't want to be honest with you.

00:24:11.510 --> 00:24:13.659
You don't have to be a doctor.

00:24:13.659 --> 00:24:15.090
I am not sure.

00:24:15.090 --> 00:24:16.450
I don't know what you're talking about.

00:24:16.450 --> 00:24:18.059
I want to see you too.

00:24:18.059 --> 00:24:19.690
What do you mean?

00:24:19.690 --> 00:24:21.910
I'm sure you wouldn't even touch me.

00:24:21.910 --> 00:24:26.230
I don't know what you're talking about.

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:30.490
Principle is completely constructed of the
same time.

00:24:30.490 --> 00:24:31.559
It's all about you to be true.

00:24:31.559 --> 00:24:33.950
You didn't even watch the movie with the rest
of the base.

00:24:33.950 --> 00:24:34.950
I don't know.

00:24:34.950 --> 00:24:35.950
I don't care.

00:24:35.950 --> 00:24:37.940
I know it's a consequence.

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:44.910
Whatever you need to know about the presence
of the story, I'm a little bit of a boy on

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:47.740
the floor.

00:24:47.740 --> 00:24:49.290
I don't know.

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:54.120
I need you to explain to me what you say.

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:55.290
What do you mean?

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:58.429
Because I don't know what you're talking about.

00:24:58.429 --> 00:24:59.690
That?

00:24:59.690 --> 00:25:05.990
That was all the time.

00:25:05.990 --> 00:25:13.549
Would have been a good time.

00:25:13.549 --> 00:25:15.670
It's a little uneven right now.

00:25:15.670 --> 00:25:19.789
This is, again, the present right now and
maybe a little bit of what we can expect in

00:25:19.789 --> 00:25:20.980
the next few years.

00:25:20.980 --> 00:25:27.929
What I want to move into now, which is really
the mind blowing stuff, is where this is going.

00:25:27.929 --> 00:25:33.451
Max, what is artificial general intelligence
and how is it different than what we have

00:25:33.451 --> 00:25:34.451
now?

00:25:34.451 --> 00:25:40.519
Yeah, if we can have this picture up here,
I'll explain how I like to think about this.

00:25:40.519 --> 00:25:45.460
I like to think about this question in terms
of this abstract landscape of tasks where

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:51.059
the elevation represents how hard it is for
AI to do each task at human level and the

00:25:51.059 --> 00:25:54.770
sea level represents what AI can do today.

00:25:54.770 --> 00:25:59.480
The sea level is obviously rising so there's
a kind of global warming going on here in

00:25:59.480 --> 00:26:00.559
the task landscape.

00:26:00.559 --> 00:26:05.610
The obvious take away from this is you should
avoid careers right at the waterfront of course,

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:06.770
which will soon be disrupted by automation.

00:26:06.770 --> 00:26:13.500
The much bigger question that you're going
for is, how high will the water end up rising?

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:18.919
Will it eventually submerge all land matching
human intelligence at all tasks?

00:26:18.919 --> 00:26:21.850
This is the definition of artificial general
intelligence.

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:27.899
This has been the Holy Grail of AI research
ever since its inception.

00:26:27.899 --> 00:26:33.820
Right, it's so hard to understand because
we've never experienced a world where there's

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:36.780
something that's generally intelligent on
a human level other than humans.

00:26:36.780 --> 00:26:42.419
It's going to be something different than
humans that is also smart the way humans are.

00:26:42.419 --> 00:26:46.559
That is so mindboggling that we can't apply
our own experience and say, "Well, it might

00:26:46.559 --> 00:26:48.750
be something like that."

00:26:48.750 --> 00:26:50.980
It's going to be very hard for us to even
imagine.

00:26:50.980 --> 00:26:57.320
Yann, you talk about that it's ... You almost
refer to it as hypothetical at this point.

00:26:57.320 --> 00:27:02.090
Well, not only don't we have the technology
for this, we don't even have the science,

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:06.880
so we don't know what principles the intelligent
machines at the level of human intelligence

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:09.759
will be based on.

00:27:09.759 --> 00:27:13.570
Now, we like to think of ourselves as being
generally intelligent, but we're not.

00:27:13.570 --> 00:27:15.899
We're actually very specialized as well.

00:27:15.899 --> 00:27:19.059
We're more general than, of course, all the
machines that we have, but our brains are

00:27:19.059 --> 00:27:20.139
very specialized.

00:27:20.139 --> 00:27:24.220
There's only certain things we do well and
if there's anything that experiments like

00:27:24.220 --> 00:27:28.350
AlphaGo has proved in the recent years is
that we totally suck at Go.

00:27:28.350 --> 00:27:30.629
We're really bad at Go.

00:27:30.629 --> 00:27:38.370
The stupid machine can beat us by a very,
very large margin.

00:27:38.370 --> 00:27:42.139
We're not very good at exploring trees, for
example, of options because we don't have

00:27:42.139 --> 00:27:43.620
that much memory.

00:27:43.620 --> 00:27:48.460
There's a lot of tasks like this that ... We're
not very good at planning a path from a city

00:27:48.460 --> 00:27:49.460
to another.

00:27:49.460 --> 00:27:53.690
This algorithm that runs on your GPS is much
better at this than you are.

00:27:53.690 --> 00:27:55.690
There are things like this that we're not
particularly good at.

00:27:55.690 --> 00:28:00.429
We know how to do them somehow, but our brains
are somewhat specialized.

00:28:00.429 --> 00:28:05.919
Now, the thing is, you were talking about
a new species, AI being very different from

00:28:05.919 --> 00:28:06.919
human intelligence.

00:28:06.919 --> 00:28:15.860
It will be very different from human intelligence
and there is one, it's sort of a trap, that

00:28:15.860 --> 00:28:20.351
is very easy to fall into which is to assume
that when machines will be intelligent, they

00:28:20.351 --> 00:28:27.590
will have all the side effects if you want,
all the characteristics of human intelligence.

00:28:27.590 --> 00:28:29.010
They will not.

00:28:29.010 --> 00:28:37.110
For example, there is the traditional Terminator
scenario that we've all heard about of machines

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:40.530
will become super intelligent and then they
will want to take over the world and kill

00:28:40.530 --> 00:28:44.899
us all.There's a lot of people who have been
claiming this is going to happen and it's

00:28:44.899 --> 00:28:48.590
inevitable and blah, blah, blah, or at least
it's a definite danger.

00:28:48.590 --> 00:28:57.840
Now, the thing is, even in the human species,
the desire to take over is not actually correlated

00:28:57.840 --> 00:28:58.840
with intelligence.

00:28:58.840 --> 00:28:59.840
It's true.

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:08.500
That is true.

00:29:08.500 --> 00:29:15.639
It's not like the people who are in leadership
positions are necessarily the smartest.

00:29:15.639 --> 00:29:22.629
In fact, there is an evolutionary argument
for the fact that it's if you are stupid that

00:29:22.629 --> 00:29:25.680
you want to be the chief.

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:29.809
Because if you are smart to survive on your
own, you don't need to convince anybody to

00:29:29.809 --> 00:29:36.840
help you, but if you are stupid, you need
everybody else to help you feed you essentially.

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:39.080
The desire to take over is not correlated
with intelligence.

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:41.120
It's correlated with testosterone probably.

00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:42.120
Yeah.

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:44.019
Tim, if I may just add a little bit to what
I said.

00:29:44.019 --> 00:29:49.750
I completely agree with you, Yann, of course,
that the Terminator stuff is silly and absolutely

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:53.299
not something that we should worry about,
but I think it's worth emphasizing a little

00:29:53.299 --> 00:29:59.860
bit more why, nonetheless, artificial general
intelligence is such a big deal if we ever

00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:01.250
get there.

00:30:01.250 --> 00:30:06.380
First of all, it's important to remember that
intelligence can give power.

00:30:06.380 --> 00:30:12.070
If you had artificial general intelligence
and you are, for example, Google, you could

00:30:12.070 --> 00:30:17.650
replace your 40,000 engineers by 40,000 AIs
that could work much faster and didn't have

00:30:17.650 --> 00:30:19.970
to take breaks.

00:30:19.970 --> 00:30:25.429
Before too long, you could be incredibly rich
and powerful and start having a vast amount

00:30:25.429 --> 00:30:27.780
of real power in the physical world.

00:30:27.780 --> 00:30:33.429
In that sense, it gives great power.

00:30:33.429 --> 00:30:41.840
Then, you can ask the question even if the
AI doesn't, as in sci-fi movies itself, somehow

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:46.649
break out and take over, do we want whatever
humans happen to be controlling the first

00:30:46.649 --> 00:30:52.690
AGI to unelected be able to take power over
the planet or would we like this power to

00:30:52.690 --> 00:30:55.110
be shared more broadly?

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:57.049
That's one example of why it's such a big
deal.

00:30:57.049 --> 00:31:03.559
A second example of why AGI, I think, would
be a huge deal is because even though I completely

00:31:03.559 --> 00:31:10.600
agree with you, Yann, that we humans are very
dumb and my teenage sons remind of this very

00:31:10.600 --> 00:31:14.549
often that I'm very dumb, there's so much
that we can't do You might think there's nothing

00:31:14.549 --> 00:31:19.019
special about human intelligence in the grand
scheme of things, but there is actually.

00:31:19.019 --> 00:31:24.010
Because in the evolution of Earth, we have
exactly just barely reached the level we are

00:31:24.010 --> 00:31:30.490
able to develop technology that might be able
to supersede us.

00:31:30.490 --> 00:31:34.870
If we have machines, which can do everything
we can, they will then perhaps also be able

00:31:34.870 --> 00:31:36.970
to be used to develop ever better machines.

00:31:36.970 --> 00:31:41.759
It's still better and that can enable AI to
bootstrap itself to become not just a tiny

00:31:41.759 --> 00:31:44.350
bit smarter than us, but way smarter.

00:31:44.350 --> 00:31:50.259
That leads to this whole controversial discussion
about an intelligence explosion, singularity

00:31:50.259 --> 00:31:52.779
and so on that's also very controversial.

00:31:52.779 --> 00:31:58.860
Those are the two reasons why I feel that
AGI would be such a huge deal even though

00:31:58.860 --> 00:32:01.279
I agree with what you said.

00:32:01.279 --> 00:32:04.490
Let's also bring in, I think it's an elephant
in the room, any time you're talking about

00:32:04.490 --> 00:32:14.789
human level of beyond intelligent computers,
consciousness.

00:32:14.789 --> 00:32:19.009
Of all the different debates in AI that are
hugely controversial, this is probably the

00:32:19.009 --> 00:32:20.009
most.

00:32:20.009 --> 00:32:23.379
You have people all over the place.

00:32:23.379 --> 00:32:29.740
Let's just define consciousness so we can
all be on the same page.

00:32:29.740 --> 00:32:31.480
Susan, what is consciousness to you?

00:32:31.480 --> 00:32:34.110
Well, it's the felt quality of experience.

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:37.500
Right now, it feels like something from the
inside to be you.

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:42.059
Every moment of your waking life and even
when you're dreaming, you are experiencing

00:32:42.059 --> 00:32:43.620
the world.

00:32:43.620 --> 00:32:46.929
Consciousness needs to be distinguished from
conscious.

00:32:46.929 --> 00:32:52.149
A lot of people run them together at first
when they're first thinking about it.

00:32:52.149 --> 00:32:56.740
To have a conscious is entirely different
than having that felt quality.

00:32:56.740 --> 00:33:01.549
That's just what it is to be alert and alive.

00:33:01.549 --> 00:33:06.309
When you see the rich hues of a sunset, when
you smell the aroma of your morning coffee,

00:33:06.309 --> 00:33:08.740
you're having conscious experience.

00:33:08.740 --> 00:33:13.980
I completely agree that consciousness is a
subjective experience.

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:23.539
It's nothing else than that, but it's very
special in that it is a domain of highly precompiled

00:33:23.539 --> 00:33:27.929
representations over which mental operators
can operate.

00:33:27.929 --> 00:33:31.879
The key operator, I think is attention, especially
volitional attention.

00:33:31.879 --> 00:33:35.039
You might have locked-in syndrome and you
could shift your volitional attention to the

00:33:35.039 --> 00:33:39.249
radio or the TV, so you'd even then have a
kind of volitional control even in this domain

00:33:39.249 --> 00:33:41.240
of your consciousness.

00:33:41.240 --> 00:33:42.749
Consciousness is for something.

00:33:42.749 --> 00:33:45.820
It's for these planning areas to have a world.

00:33:45.820 --> 00:33:50.960
In one sense, it's a veridical hallucination,
but it's not a hallucination because it's

00:33:50.960 --> 00:33:56.940
not saying what's not there or it's saying
what is there.

00:33:56.940 --> 00:33:59.179
It allows us to act in this world.

00:33:59.179 --> 00:34:00.289
That's only half of consciousness.

00:34:00.289 --> 00:34:02.649
The other half of consciousness is imagination.

00:34:02.649 --> 00:34:07.840
If I were to buzz you, probably about half
of you right now are zoning out and thinking

00:34:07.840 --> 00:34:15.300
about this or that, but we spend about half
of our lives in this imaginary virtual reality

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:16.879
or our own creation.

00:34:16.879 --> 00:34:18.440
In this domain, we have total freedom.

00:34:18.440 --> 00:34:23.080
We can do anything and then we can go and
build it in the world if we want.

00:34:23.080 --> 00:34:27.060
Consciousness is for something and it takes
quite a while to make it.

00:34:27.060 --> 00:34:31.350
The photons in the world hit your retina at
time zero, your consciousness is not happening

00:34:31.350 --> 00:34:32.350
at time zero.

00:34:32.350 --> 00:34:36.659
There's a lot of processing that goes on in
the first quarter to a third of a second and

00:34:36.659 --> 00:34:42.560
then you experience a full-blown world that
allows you then to act in the world.

00:34:42.560 --> 00:34:47.580
Yeah, I share the definition that you both
gave of consciousness as subjective experience.

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:52.980
When I drive down the street, I'm experiencing
colors and sounds and vibrations and motions,

00:34:52.980 --> 00:34:56.429
but does the self-driving car experience anything?

00:34:56.429 --> 00:35:03.589
That's a question I think we honestly don't
have a good scientific answer for yet.

00:35:03.589 --> 00:35:05.571
I love how controversial this is.

00:35:05.571 --> 00:35:10.070
If you look up the word consciousness in the
Macmillan Dictionary of Psychology from a

00:35:10.070 --> 00:35:14.339
few years back, it says nothing of interest
has ever been written on the subject.

00:35:14.339 --> 00:35:21.920
Even when I asked a lot of science colleagues,
most of them say, "Consciousness is just B.S."

00:35:21.920 --> 00:35:27.780
When I ask them why, I notice that they form
the two camps that disagree violently with

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:29.440
each other about why it's B.S.

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:33.730
Half of them say it's B.S. because, of course,
machines can't be conscious.

00:35:33.730 --> 00:35:36.660
You have to be made of meat to be conscious.

00:35:36.660 --> 00:35:43.230
Then, the other half says, "Of course, this
is B.S. because consciousness and intelligence

00:35:43.230 --> 00:35:44.400
are just the same thing."

00:35:44.400 --> 00:35:52.430
In other words, anything that acts as if it
were conscious will be conscious.

00:35:52.430 --> 00:35:56.331
To be contrarian, to most of my colleagues,
I think the truth is probably somewhere in

00:35:56.331 --> 00:36:01.970
between because I know that most of the information
processing in my brain I'm actually not conscious

00:36:01.970 --> 00:36:05.660
of, the heartbeat regulation and the vast
majority of other things.

00:36:05.660 --> 00:36:11.500
Actually, when I look up and be like, "Oh,
there is Yann," I have no idea how all that

00:36:11.500 --> 00:36:13.290
information processing happens.

00:36:13.290 --> 00:36:17.700
What I'm aware of is just this CEO part of
my brain that gets emailed the end result

00:36:17.700 --> 00:36:22.790
of the computation.

00:36:22.790 --> 00:36:27.500
Not only do I think it's not a B.S. question,
I think people who have been saying for so

00:36:27.500 --> 00:36:34.319
long it's a B.S. question have actually been
lame and just running away from a genuine

00:36:34.319 --> 00:36:35.369
science question.

00:36:35.369 --> 00:36:39.160
'Cause usually if you have a great science
question that lingers for hundreds of years

00:36:39.160 --> 00:36:43.000
it's because people just dismissed it rather
than doing the hard work.

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:45.890
I think we need to do the hard work on this.

00:36:45.890 --> 00:36:49.640
If you're a physician in the emergency room
and you have an unresponsive patient coming

00:36:49.640 --> 00:36:54.630
in, wouldn't it be great to have a consciousness
detector that can tell you whether this person

00:36:54.630 --> 00:36:58.880
is in a coma and there's one home or whether
they have locked-in syndrome?

00:36:58.880 --> 00:37:03.870
If you have a helper robot, wouldn't you want
to know if it's conscious so you should feel

00:37:03.870 --> 00:37:06.020
guilty about switching it off?

00:37:06.020 --> 00:37:11.980
Or, whether it's just like a zombie so you
should feel creeped out when it's pretending

00:37:11.980 --> 00:37:15.089
to be happy about what you said?

00:37:15.089 --> 00:37:19.510
I'd like to know when we do these things.

00:37:19.510 --> 00:37:28.230
The question of consciousness is probably
not posed properly in the sense that back

00:37:28.230 --> 00:37:36.130
in the 18th century or 17th century or even
perhaps earlier when a scientist discovered

00:37:36.130 --> 00:37:41.670
how the eye works and that the image on the
retina forms upside down.

00:37:41.670 --> 00:37:44.600
They were baffled by the fact that we see
right-side up.

00:37:44.600 --> 00:37:48.820
How is it that we don't see upside down because
the image in the back of our eyes upside down?

00:37:48.820 --> 00:37:49.820
It was a big mystery.

00:37:49.820 --> 00:37:53.970
Now that we know what information processing
is all about, we think this question makes

00:37:53.970 --> 00:37:55.790
absolutely no sense.

00:37:55.790 --> 00:37:57.430
The whole statement makes no sense.

00:37:57.430 --> 00:38:01.089
I think there are things about consciousness
of that nature that we're not asking the right

00:38:01.089 --> 00:38:07.380
question, but there's a lot of contrarian
opinions on this that I'd be happy to take

00:38:07.380 --> 00:38:12.250
at any moment not totally seriously because
I don't completely believe in them.

00:38:12.250 --> 00:38:17.020
The fact, for example, that consciousness
is an epi-phenomenon of being intelligent.

00:38:17.020 --> 00:38:21.550
So, any intelligent entity will have to be
conscious because they will have to have some

00:38:21.550 --> 00:38:22.910
sort of model of itself.

00:38:22.910 --> 00:38:28.140
That's, according to some definition, that
satisfies consciousness.

00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:34.109
There's another one that I like which I connect
with, maybe other people connect with it as

00:38:34.109 --> 00:38:39.570
well, which is consciousness is actually a
consequence of our brains being very limited.

00:38:39.570 --> 00:38:43.880
We can only focus our attention on one thing
at a time and therefore ... That's because

00:38:43.880 --> 00:38:45.559
our brain is limited hardware.

00:38:45.559 --> 00:38:51.089
We have our prefrontal cortex that has to
focus on one task or one particular situation

00:38:51.089 --> 00:38:53.200
and cannot do multiple things at the same
time.

00:38:53.200 --> 00:38:58.040
We have to have a process in our brain that
decides what to pay attention to and how to

00:38:58.040 --> 00:39:03.020
configure our prefrontal cortex to solve the
problem at hand.

00:39:03.020 --> 00:39:06.250
We interpret this as consciousness, but it's
just the consequence of the fact that our

00:39:06.250 --> 00:39:10.890
brain is so small, that if our brain was ten
times the size, then we could do ten things

00:39:10.890 --> 00:39:14.780
at the same time and maybe we wouldn't have
the same experience of consciousness.

00:39:14.780 --> 00:39:16.300
Maybe we will have ten simultaneous consciousnesses.

00:39:16.300 --> 00:39:19.710
Is there a plural for consciousness?

00:39:19.710 --> 00:39:20.710
Is it consciousnesses?

00:39:20.710 --> 00:39:21.710
Consciousnesses.

00:39:21.710 --> 00:39:22.710
Let's go with that.

00:39:22.710 --> 00:39:23.710
Okay.

00:39:23.710 --> 00:39:25.950
It's not a collective word, is it?

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:27.740
Yeah.

00:39:27.740 --> 00:39:28.740
I thought-

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:32.640
I think we just don't know enough really to
ask these kinds of questions.

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:36.000
Let's start with Peter and then we'll go to
Susan.

00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:40.390
All right, so bringing it back a little bit
to the question of artificial intelligence,

00:39:40.390 --> 00:39:45.010
I think that why did consciousness evolve?

00:39:45.010 --> 00:39:46.010
Well, it's for something.

00:39:46.010 --> 00:39:49.050
It's for the frontal areas to be able to plan.

00:39:49.050 --> 00:39:52.110
You want to get the best representation of
the world that you can.

00:39:52.110 --> 00:39:58.160
Now, in order to do that you need to take
incredibly ambiguous visual input and recover

00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:04.760
a disambiguated representation of the world
so the areas can plan appropriately.

00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:07.020
Let's say I have a white-haired cat.

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:09.651
It looks white to me because I want to recover
what's intrinsically true about the cat, namely

00:40:09.651 --> 00:40:12.610
that it's a white-haired cat.

00:40:12.610 --> 00:40:14.970
Now it runs under a shadow or a blue light.

00:40:14.970 --> 00:40:18.380
Well, the light actually reflecting off of
the white hair is now blue, what's hitting

00:40:18.380 --> 00:40:22.270
my retina, but I want to discount that and
recover what's still intrinsically true, so

00:40:22.270 --> 00:40:26.339
I see it as a white cat that happens to be
under a blue light.

00:40:26.339 --> 00:40:30.750
I want to recover it's intrinsically true
shape and size and distance and so forth.

00:40:30.750 --> 00:40:33.780
It's the best representation of what's intrinsically
the case.

00:40:33.780 --> 00:40:40.349
Again, what got built into this quasi-hallucination
is, in addition to that kind of story about

00:40:40.349 --> 00:40:45.099
the physical world, stories like causation
which is invisible.

00:40:45.099 --> 00:40:50.860
Go to any party, next time you're at a party,
and have your confederate turn off the lights

00:40:50.860 --> 00:40:54.300
and you say, "I can turn the lights off,"
and you go, boom.

00:40:54.300 --> 00:40:55.829
The person turns the lights off.

00:40:55.829 --> 00:40:57.780
Everyone's like, "Wow, how did you do that?"

00:40:57.780 --> 00:41:00.740
Because we are perceiving causation.

00:41:00.740 --> 00:41:02.359
We're also perceiving other minds.

00:41:02.359 --> 00:41:04.900
It's built into the construction.

00:41:04.900 --> 00:41:09.059
My guess is, that this is going to be very
central to the creation ultimately of AGI

00:41:09.059 --> 00:41:13.970
or general intelligence 'cause it's so central
to the creation of our models of the world.

00:41:13.970 --> 00:41:18.589
I understand what it's like for you to feel
pain because I feel pain or for you to have

00:41:18.589 --> 00:41:20.859
a broken heart because I once did.

00:41:20.859 --> 00:41:21.859
This is very central.

00:41:21.859 --> 00:41:26.690
I don't see how a system that has never felt
pain can understand what I mean when I'm talking

00:41:26.690 --> 00:41:27.690
about pain.

00:41:27.690 --> 00:41:28.690
Susan.

00:41:28.690 --> 00:41:29.930
That's interesting.

00:41:29.930 --> 00:41:37.700
I guess my general comment here, to go back
to Yann's point about how attention is closely

00:41:37.700 --> 00:41:44.200
related to consciousness and we could have
got lucky because we have limited capacity.

00:41:44.200 --> 00:41:49.280
We can only entertain maybe seven variables
in working memory at any given time, and we

00:41:49.280 --> 00:41:51.460
have trouble remembering phone numbers.

00:41:51.460 --> 00:41:58.250
Maybe consciousness is something we got that
relates to our limited capacity systems.

00:41:58.250 --> 00:42:04.880
Now, if that's true though, suppose we do
create AGI and shortly thereafter we create

00:42:04.880 --> 00:42:10.609
intelligent synthetic beings that are smarter
than us in all sorts of ways, why think they're

00:42:10.609 --> 00:42:11.609
conscious?

00:42:11.609 --> 00:42:16.990
Just because they look like, say, Hanson Robotics
Sophia, they look human, does mean that they'll

00:42:16.990 --> 00:42:17.990
be conscious.

00:42:17.990 --> 00:42:18.990
Think about it.

00:42:18.990 --> 00:42:21.770
Do they need to have these limited capacity
systems?

00:42:21.770 --> 00:42:27.270
For example, a superintelligence could be
as large as an entire planet.

00:42:27.270 --> 00:42:32.520
Its computronium, its computational resources
could span the entire internet.

00:42:32.520 --> 00:42:36.060
What would be novel to it requiring slow,
deliberative focus?

00:42:36.060 --> 00:42:42.390
Why would it be like us in any kind of meaningful
sense?

00:42:42.390 --> 00:42:48.390
What I want to suggest is that we pull apart
intelligence and consciousness and treat it

00:42:48.390 --> 00:42:49.800
as an empirical matter.

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:56.230
If we want to figure out machine consciousness,
we need to ask for each type of AI architecture

00:42:56.230 --> 00:43:01.059
whether that type of system has conscious
experience and not just assume that because

00:43:01.059 --> 00:43:03.049
it looks human it feels something.

00:43:03.049 --> 00:43:09.450
Yeah, I want to applaud you there for distinguishing
it's an artificial intelligence and artificial

00:43:09.450 --> 00:43:12.450
consciousness, which are way too often conflated
with each other.

00:43:12.450 --> 00:43:18.460
I think many people, for example, will say
things like, "Oh, we're so scared that machines

00:43:18.460 --> 00:43:21.930
are going to become conscious and then suddenly
they're going to turn on us and be evil like

00:43:21.930 --> 00:43:23.109
in bad Hollywood movies."

00:43:23.109 --> 00:43:26.190
Somehow, it's the consciousness that you should
worry about.

00:43:26.190 --> 00:43:28.540
That, I think, is a total red herring.

00:43:28.540 --> 00:43:32.980
Although I agree that consciousness is super
important from a moral and ethical point of

00:43:32.980 --> 00:43:33.980
view-

00:43:33.980 --> 00:43:34.980
Yeah, of course.

00:43:34.980 --> 00:43:37.680
... in terms of whether you should worry or
not, you don't care about whether that heat-seeking

00:43:37.680 --> 00:43:43.100
missile chasing after you is conscious or
not or how it feels about this.

00:43:43.100 --> 00:43:48.630
You only care about what it does and it's
perfectly possible for us to get in trouble

00:43:48.630 --> 00:43:56.380
with some incredibly intelligent machine even
if it's not having any subjective experience.

00:43:56.380 --> 00:44:00.109
In other words, consciousness isn't something
we should worry about.

00:44:00.109 --> 00:44:08.339
That's not going to make any particular difference
from that perspective, but I think it makes

00:44:08.339 --> 00:44:10.190
enormous moral difference.

00:44:10.190 --> 00:44:14.740
When I have colleagues who tell me that they
think we shouldn't talk about consciousness

00:44:14.740 --> 00:44:20.049
because it's just philosophical B.S., I ask
them to explain to me how you can have any

00:44:20.049 --> 00:44:25.690
morality if you refuse to talk about consciousness
and subjective experience.

00:44:25.690 --> 00:44:29.619
What's wrong with torture if it's just, oh,
the elementary particles were moving around

00:44:29.619 --> 00:44:32.069
this way rather than that way?

00:44:32.069 --> 00:44:36.730
It's all about the negativity of the subjective
experience that's at hand.

00:44:36.730 --> 00:44:40.740
If we want to be moral people, we want to
create a lot of positive experiences in the

00:44:40.740 --> 00:44:44.770
future, not just a bunch of zombies.

00:44:44.770 --> 00:44:46.170
This is a Nick Bostrom example.

00:44:46.170 --> 00:44:50.680
If there's a trillion simulations you're running
just to test something of a general intelligent

00:44:50.680 --> 00:44:52.730
trillion, then you're like, "Okay, I got what
I needed.

00:44:52.730 --> 00:44:54.450
The inflows, let's shut them all off."

00:44:54.450 --> 00:44:56.339
If they're not conscious, it's like closing
your laptop.

00:44:56.339 --> 00:44:57.490
There's nothing wrong with that.

00:44:57.490 --> 00:45:01.809
If those things are conscious, you just created
the biggest genocide in the history of the

00:45:01.809 --> 00:45:02.809
human species.

00:45:02.809 --> 00:45:03.809
It's pretty relevant.

00:45:03.809 --> 00:45:04.809
It matters.

00:45:04.809 --> 00:45:06.309
Not if you have a backup.

00:45:06.309 --> 00:45:10.450
The reason why we care about each other is
because we have a lot invested in each other.

00:45:10.450 --> 00:45:15.570
There is value to every human particularly
through other humans who are close to that

00:45:15.570 --> 00:45:23.839
person It's possible that we'll have the same
relationship with our household robot that

00:45:23.839 --> 00:45:25.010
we trained.

00:45:25.010 --> 00:45:30.770
We have a lot invested in that household robot,
the same that we have invested in our cat

00:45:30.770 --> 00:45:34.089
or dogs.

00:45:34.089 --> 00:45:39.140
We won't want that robot to get destroyed
because all of the time we invested in that

00:45:39.140 --> 00:45:40.480
robot will go away.

00:45:40.480 --> 00:45:46.940
But, if we have a backup, it's okay to smash
it against the wall.

00:45:46.940 --> 00:45:49.760
If you have an identical twin, can I just
throw you into the sewer?

00:45:49.760 --> 00:45:57.990
No, there is all kinds of interesting questions
like this of imagine we invent ... We have

00:45:57.990 --> 00:45:59.260
a physicist here.

00:45:59.260 --> 00:46:03.750
We invent a Star Trek style transporter.

00:46:03.750 --> 00:46:04.819
You get dematerialized.

00:46:04.819 --> 00:46:05.819
You get destroyed.

00:46:05.819 --> 00:46:10.290
You get killed and you get reconstructed at
the other end.

00:46:10.290 --> 00:46:12.940
You experience death.

00:46:12.940 --> 00:46:21.750
This is a metaphor really for what is it that
we're upset at when someone gets killed or

00:46:21.750 --> 00:46:26.510
when an intelligent machine with its conscious
gets destroyed?

00:46:26.510 --> 00:46:31.849
As long as there's no pain involved, which
there isn't when you go to anesthesia.

00:46:31.849 --> 00:46:36.450
As long as you have a backup or you can get
revived, there is no-

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:37.450
But, if there's suffering-

00:46:37.450 --> 00:46:38.450
... no information loss.

00:46:38.450 --> 00:46:40.630
If there's suffering then that's a different
thing.

00:46:40.630 --> 00:46:41.630
Yep.

00:46:41.630 --> 00:46:42.630
That's right.

00:46:42.630 --> 00:46:43.630
Then, consciousness does ...

00:46:43.630 --> 00:46:45.770
Okay, so now I ask the question, can machines
have emotions?

00:46:45.770 --> 00:46:52.490
You see, again, Star Trek Commander Data has
this chip they can turn on or off to have

00:46:52.490 --> 00:46:57.680
emotions or not 'cause somehow you have intelligent
machines that don't have emotions.

00:46:57.680 --> 00:47:04.069
I don't personally believe that it is possible
to design or build autonomous intelligent

00:47:04.069 --> 00:47:06.460
machines without them having emotions.

00:47:06.460 --> 00:47:08.369
Emotion is part of intelligence.

00:47:08.369 --> 00:47:12.680
Now, we're going to have self-driving cars
that are not going to have much emotions,

00:47:12.680 --> 00:47:15.750
but it's because they're not going to be,
even though we call them autonomous cars,

00:47:15.750 --> 00:47:17.270
they're not going to be autonomous intelligence.

00:47:17.270 --> 00:47:18.630
They're just designed to drive your car.

00:47:18.630 --> 00:47:25.829
If you're talking about autonomous intelligence,
then these are machines that can decide what

00:47:25.829 --> 00:47:26.829
they do.

00:47:26.829 --> 00:47:34.160
They have some intrinsic drive that makes
them wake up every morning or do particular

00:47:34.160 --> 00:47:44.430
things, justify their lives maybe, but no
pre-programmed behavior really.

00:47:44.430 --> 00:47:46.680
You can't have a machine like this without
emotions.

00:47:46.680 --> 00:47:47.680
]

00:47:47.680 --> 00:47:48.680
Peter.

00:47:48.680 --> 00:47:51.560
Yeah, so I think it's a very interesting point
that emotions are going to prove central to

00:47:51.560 --> 00:47:54.740
the generation of artificial general intelligence.

00:47:54.740 --> 00:47:59.890
If you look at the evolution of animals, we
can learn something, I think, about the origin

00:47:59.890 --> 00:48:05.630
of the emotions and the desires because they
are conscious states but they're teleological

00:48:05.630 --> 00:48:10.550
states within consciousness and they're often
about what's not visible.

00:48:10.550 --> 00:48:11.550
How would this get started?

00:48:11.550 --> 00:48:16.380
Well, you could imagine a fish that only responded
to something it could see.

00:48:16.380 --> 00:48:18.119
It's stimulus present, it does this.

00:48:18.119 --> 00:48:23.990
If it sees a barracuda, it flees.Then, imagine
a new revolutionary fish that has a working

00:48:23.990 --> 00:48:24.990
memory.

00:48:24.990 --> 00:48:28.700
Now, when the barracuda peers behind a piece
of coral, that fish can say, "A-ha!

00:48:28.700 --> 00:48:30.930
I know it's going that way.

00:48:30.930 --> 00:48:32.329
I'm going to go that way."

00:48:32.329 --> 00:48:34.540
The representation of the invisible became,
I think, very central.

00:48:34.540 --> 00:48:38.690
The need for working memory is very central,
which is lacking in present architectures.

00:48:38.690 --> 00:48:46.079
Then, these teleological states that force
us to seek mates and food and so forth and

00:48:46.079 --> 00:48:52.599
really having these teleological states, these
emotions and desires, allowed us to form,

00:48:52.599 --> 00:48:54.089
not garden paths, but desert paths.

00:48:54.089 --> 00:48:58.549
A garden path is when you know locally this
is best, locally this is best, locally this

00:48:58.549 --> 00:49:00.900
is best and then you end up in the jaws of
a lion.

00:49:00.900 --> 00:49:04.049
A desert path would be well locally I have
to go without, I go without, I go without,

00:49:04.049 --> 00:49:07.799
but at the end of it, I might have a mate
or food or shelter.

00:49:07.799 --> 00:49:12.480
This is a big revolution that afforded us
the ability to act in the world in the absence

00:49:12.480 --> 00:49:15.030
of input.

00:49:15.030 --> 00:49:20.339
Central to that also is the formation of mental
models and cognitive maps of the whole landscape,

00:49:20.339 --> 00:49:22.819
physical and emotional as well as social.

00:49:22.819 --> 00:49:27.119
Actually, one of the big progress, a very
interesting development in deep learning over

00:49:27.119 --> 00:49:32.740
the last years, is deep learning systems that
have a working memory, neural networks, neural

00:49:32.740 --> 00:49:35.170
Turing machines, things like that.

00:49:35.170 --> 00:49:41.069
Those are models that actually have a separate
module for competition and another one for

00:49:41.069 --> 00:49:44.210
storing memory, short-term memory.

00:49:44.210 --> 00:49:50.550
Similar to we actually have a particular module
in our brains called the hippocampus which

00:49:50.550 --> 00:49:54.260
sort of plays that role more or less of storing
short-term memory.

00:49:54.260 --> 00:49:58.630
I think a very interesting place to look for
lessons about how to build artificial intelligence

00:49:58.630 --> 00:50:02.760
systems will be not computers, but evolution's
other experiments.

00:50:02.760 --> 00:50:08.750
I think the most interesting one is the octopus
because complex brains evolved in three lineages,

00:50:08.750 --> 00:50:13.260
the chordates, and we're sort of the culmination
of that 'cause were like chimps plus symbolic

00:50:13.260 --> 00:50:14.760
processing plus syntax.

00:50:14.760 --> 00:50:19.100
Then, some arthropods like praying mantises,
but honeybees, they have a couple hundred-thousand

00:50:19.100 --> 00:50:20.100
neurons.

00:50:20.100 --> 00:50:27.730
The octopus has 500 million, comparable to
a bear or a dog.

00:50:27.730 --> 00:50:32.630
If we want to understand computational principles
that might be universal, we should look at

00:50:32.630 --> 00:50:36.339
this animal because there might be only so
many ways to build a brain.

00:50:36.339 --> 00:50:38.599
Convergent evolution found that there's only
so many ways to build a wing.

00:50:38.599 --> 00:50:41.171
You need some sort of membrane.

00:50:41.171 --> 00:50:46.180
In the chordates, the bats did this and the
birds did this and the pterodactyls did this,

00:50:46.180 --> 00:50:48.130
but they all have in common flapping and membranes.

00:50:48.130 --> 00:50:50.000
There's only so many ways to build a wing.

00:50:50.000 --> 00:50:52.470
There might be only so many ways to build
a brain.

00:50:52.470 --> 00:50:56.510
Some people have argued, for example, that
the vertical lobe of the octopus brain is

00:50:56.510 --> 00:51:00.400
completely or very analogous to our hippocampus
with very similar circuitry.

00:51:00.400 --> 00:51:03.960
Well, convergent evolution has brought us
there 'cause our common ancestors probably

00:51:03.960 --> 00:51:04.960
Precambrian.

00:51:04.960 --> 00:51:09.890
It was probably a little flatworm way, way
back in the ancient, warm seas.

00:51:09.890 --> 00:51:15.110
That's really interesting because to go back
to this idea of superintelligence, one wonders

00:51:15.110 --> 00:51:21.289
if we could discover through thinking of both
AI and intriguing systems that nature gives

00:51:21.289 --> 00:51:26.109
us like the octopus if there are universal
properties of intelligence and, in so doing,

00:51:26.109 --> 00:51:28.890
anticipate the shape of superintelligence.

00:51:28.890 --> 00:51:29.890
Because after this panel, I have to confess,
I'm actually a little bit more worried about

00:51:29.890 --> 00:51:30.890
super intelligence
.

00:51:30.890 --> 00:51:35.359
Our basic behaviors, as humans, are driven
by our basal ganglia basically.

00:51:35.359 --> 00:51:39.810
The base of our brain, that's where human
nature is hardwired.

00:51:39.810 --> 00:51:43.200
That's what drives a lot of our basic behaviors.

00:51:43.200 --> 00:51:53.650
Then our brain on top of this makes our behavior
serve those drives with intelligent, hopefully

00:51:53.650 --> 00:52:03.230
intelligent, actions, but our basic drives
are driven by this hardwired basal ganglia.

00:52:03.230 --> 00:52:07.050
That's what computes whether we are happy
or not, whether what we do is going to make

00:52:07.050 --> 00:52:08.330
us happy or not.

00:52:08.330 --> 00:52:09.800
It drives all of our behavior.

00:52:09.800 --> 00:52:11.339
We need this for intelligent machines.

00:52:11.339 --> 00:52:15.730
The fact that an intelligent machine will
be autonomous will mean that it will have

00:52:15.730 --> 00:52:21.770
to have this kind of hardwired piece in its
brain that drives its behavior.

00:52:21.770 --> 00:52:28.039
The big question is, how do you build it in
such a way that those basic drives are aligned

00:52:28.039 --> 00:52:29.900
with human values?

00:52:29.900 --> 00:52:33.789
It's going to be probably very difficult to
hardwire this by hand.

00:52:33.789 --> 00:52:38.820
We're going to be able to hardwire some very
basic behavior to make sure that robots are

00:52:38.820 --> 00:52:39.820
safe.

00:52:39.820 --> 00:52:43.030
For example, if you have a knife in your hand,
don't flail it around if there are humans

00:52:43.030 --> 00:52:45.530
around, sort of very basic things like this.

00:52:45.530 --> 00:52:50.220
There's probably thousands of rules like this
that we can't really implement really easily.

00:52:50.220 --> 00:52:57.059
What we're going to have to do is train those
machines to, again, distinguish good from

00:52:57.059 --> 00:53:02.650
evil, behave in society and not injure people.

00:53:02.650 --> 00:53:09.550
Yeah, I hear people say it's the artificial
superintelligence, which is kind of general

00:53:09.550 --> 00:53:11.800
intelligence once it's way better than we
are.

00:53:11.800 --> 00:53:18.500
It's the last invention we'll ever make because
if it's doing what we want, then all the things

00:53:18.500 --> 00:53:23.130
that we think are hard ... It's like a monkey
hitting a padlock forever and a human walks

00:53:23.130 --> 00:53:26.290
in and they look at the instructions and in
just one second open it.

00:53:26.290 --> 00:53:33.130
That all these things, poverty, climate change,
disease, even morality, child's play to something

00:53:33.130 --> 00:53:35.490
that is that level of intelligent.

00:53:35.490 --> 00:53:39.430
It's this utopia that we could be in if we
could pull it off.

00:53:39.430 --> 00:53:43.890
So, you wouldn't have to invent anything in
that world because it invents everything for

00:53:43.890 --> 00:53:44.890
you.

00:53:44.890 --> 00:53:50.250
The other scenario is that it's ... I don't
hear a lot of experts talking about Terminator,

00:53:50.250 --> 00:53:52.680
evil robots, that's anthropomorphizing..

00:53:52.680 --> 00:53:57.020
It's the last invention we'll ever face then
'cause extinct species don't invent things.

00:53:57.020 --> 00:54:01.050
The stakes are monumentally high and this
is what you just kind of touched on

00:54:01.050 --> 00:54:02.339
We only have a few minutes left.

00:54:02.339 --> 00:54:06.170
I really want to hear what you guys have to
say about ... I feel like we woke up in the

00:54:06.170 --> 00:54:09.819
middle of a thriller movie in the climax of
this thriller movie, but it's just moving

00:54:09.819 --> 00:54:14.540
slowly in our minds so we don't see that what's
happening, but it's the choose-your-own-adventure,

00:54:14.540 --> 00:54:16.420
choose-your-own-ending.

00:54:16.420 --> 00:54:18.520
How can we nudge this in the right direction?

00:54:18.520 --> 00:54:19.520
Yeah.

00:54:19.520 --> 00:54:26.520
If you're taking a big step back and looking
at it after 13.8 billion years of cosmic history,

00:54:26.520 --> 00:54:30.200
here we are, we figured out how to replace
most of our muscle work by machines.

00:54:30.200 --> 00:54:31.619
That was the industrial revolution.

00:54:31.619 --> 00:54:35.190
Now, we're figuring out how to replace our
mental work by machines.

00:54:35.190 --> 00:54:36.520
Eventually, that's going to be AGI superintelligence.

00:54:36.520 --> 00:54:38.730
So, how can we make it good?

00:54:38.730 --> 00:54:44.380
I think Yann mentioned that the key challenge
there about making sure that its goals are

00:54:44.380 --> 00:54:48.500
aligned with ours, it doesn't have to be a
bad news being surrounded by more intelligent

00:54:48.500 --> 00:54:53.660
entities because we all did that when we were
two years old, mommy and daddy.

00:54:53.660 --> 00:54:57.840
It worked out for us because their goals were
aligned with ours.

00:54:57.840 --> 00:55:01.210
How can we ensure that this will happen with
AGI?

00:55:01.210 --> 00:55:04.619
Well, AI safety research is the answer.

00:55:04.619 --> 00:55:10.750
We're investing billions of dollars now into
making AI more powerful, but we also have

00:55:10.750 --> 00:55:16.640
to invest money in developing the wisdom needed
to keep this AI beneficial.

00:55:16.640 --> 00:55:20.079
For example, applicable to what you said,
Yann, I think we have to figure out how to

00:55:20.079 --> 00:55:27.010
make machines understand our goals, adopt
our goals, and retain our goals as they get

00:55:27.010 --> 00:55:28.010
smarter.

00:55:28.010 --> 00:55:29.010
All of those are really tough.

00:55:29.010 --> 00:55:33.540
If you tell your future self-driving Uber
to take you to JFK as fast as possible and

00:55:33.540 --> 00:55:38.170
you get there covered in vomit and chased
by helicopters, "No, no, no, no.

00:55:38.170 --> 00:55:40.539
This isn't what I asked for."

00:55:40.539 --> 00:55:44.180
And, it goes, "that's exactly what you asked
for."

00:55:44.180 --> 00:55:48.240
Then, you appreciate how hard it is to make
machines understand our real goal.

00:55:48.240 --> 00:55:51.730
Raise your hand if you have kids.

00:55:51.730 --> 00:55:56.400
Then, you know how big the difference is between
having them understand your goals and actually

00:55:56.400 --> 00:55:58.320
adopting your goals, doing what you want.

00:55:58.320 --> 00:56:00.020
Also, who's the parent deciding what the goals
are?

00:56:00.020 --> 00:56:01.020
Well, in this case-

00:56:01.020 --> 00:56:02.390
ISIS thinks it's doing good.

00:56:02.390 --> 00:56:03.550
It does
.

00:56:03.550 --> 00:56:04.550
Yeah.

00:56:04.550 --> 00:56:06.180
We put a lot of effort into raising our kids.

00:56:06.180 --> 00:56:10.789
We need to put even more effort into raising
humanity's proverbial kids if we develop ever

00:56:10.789 --> 00:56:12.750
machines that are more powerful than us.

00:56:12.750 --> 00:56:14.280
I actually disagree with this.

00:56:14.280 --> 00:56:15.280
Well, okay.

00:56:15.280 --> 00:56:16.289
Let's go down the line here.

00:56:16.289 --> 00:56:20.880
Some of the changes that will have to happen
will not only be on the AI side but on the

00:56:20.880 --> 00:56:24.960
cultural side, the transformation of our cultures.

00:56:24.960 --> 00:56:27.559
For example, any technology can be used for
good or evil.

00:56:27.559 --> 00:56:29.140
A hammer can kill somebody or build a house.

00:56:29.140 --> 00:56:31.780
An airplane can transport people or bomb people.

00:56:31.780 --> 00:56:38.049
This is also true of AI, but the ethical systems
that we have inherited from the past are not

00:56:38.049 --> 00:56:40.180
sufficient to deal with this.

00:56:40.180 --> 00:56:44.700
2,000 years ago there was ten bad things you
can do and they said, "Okay, God said don't

00:56:44.700 --> 00:56:50.520
sleep with his wife and don't steal his stuff,"
and so forth.

00:56:50.520 --> 00:56:57.720
Commandment number 853,211, thou shalt not
implant bioluminescent protein alleles from

00:56:57.720 --> 00:57:00.829
fireflies into tomato, no glow-in-the-dark
tomatoes.

00:57:00.829 --> 00:57:10.359
Thou shalt not raise embryos for their dopaminergic
neurons to implant into Parkinson's patients.

00:57:10.359 --> 00:57:17.109
Technology has driven ... There's now infinitely
many things that are bad, harmful so we need

00:57:17.109 --> 00:57:20.980
to come up with a new ethical framework for
figuring out the right course of action in

00:57:20.980 --> 00:57:22.890
these infinitely many cases.

00:57:22.890 --> 00:57:27.569
I would say a first step would be thinking
of what is good is that which is fostering

00:57:27.569 --> 00:57:30.900
life and especially human life, but also life
in general.

00:57:30.900 --> 00:57:34.809
That which is harmful to life is not good.

00:57:34.809 --> 00:57:39.839
That way we can confront lots of things and
try to think about not only what can we do,

00:57:39.839 --> 00:57:42.390
but what should we do.

00:57:42.390 --> 00:57:47.920
I think we're in a fortunate situation actually
where pretty much everything you can do which

00:57:47.920 --> 00:57:54.579
will increase the chances of superintelligence
or AGI going well, that kind of safety research,

00:57:54.579 --> 00:57:59.089
actually has its first baby step doing something
which is already useful and the short-term

00:57:59.089 --> 00:58:04.920
like better cybersecurity research so we don't
get hacked all the time, for example.

00:58:04.920 --> 00:58:11.000
Let's do those things better 'cause I think
we're actually pathetically flippant with

00:58:11.000 --> 00:58:18.390
things like that now and who's going to trust
your AGI if it can get hacked?

00:58:18.390 --> 00:58:23.240
We're going to get very non-powerful AGI before
we get very powerful AGIs.

00:58:23.240 --> 00:58:29.180
Our first AGI will have the autonomy and the
intelligence level of a rat if that.

00:58:29.180 --> 00:58:30.180
Okay.

00:58:30.180 --> 00:58:34.839
I considered it a major success in my career
if, by the end of my career, which is coming

00:58:34.839 --> 00:58:40.109
fast, we have a machine that has the same
level of common sense as a rat or let's say

00:58:40.109 --> 00:58:41.109
a cat.

00:58:41.109 --> 00:58:43.110
The cat has 700 million neurons.

00:58:43.110 --> 00:58:45.369
We don't have the technology for this already.

00:58:45.369 --> 00:58:48.130
We don't have the science for it.

00:58:48.130 --> 00:58:52.059
Once we figure out the design of an intelligent,
autonomous system, it will have the intelligence

00:58:52.059 --> 00:58:53.170
of a cat or a rat.

00:58:53.170 --> 00:58:55.300
It's not going to take over the world.

00:58:55.300 --> 00:59:02.150
With this, we can experiment to figure out
how do we build into it the fact that it should

00:59:02.150 --> 00:59:05.319
behave in society and not kill everything
around it.

00:59:05.319 --> 00:59:08.039
Let me just point out that ...

00:59:08.039 --> 00:59:09.809
The thing that ...

00:59:09.809 --> 00:59:10.809
I'm sorry.

00:59:10.809 --> 00:59:11.809
Go ahead.

00:59:11.809 --> 00:59:12.809
Oh, no.

00:59:12.809 --> 00:59:13.809
Go ahead.

00:59:13.809 --> 00:59:14.809
Okay, thanks.

00:59:14.809 --> 00:59:15.809
Coming out of neuroscience, we just have really
basic fundamental questions that we don't

00:59:15.809 --> 00:59:16.809
know the answer to yet.

00:59:16.809 --> 00:59:17.809
Science says it's all about what we don't
know, so we should just put this on the table.

00:59:17.809 --> 00:59:20.109
One of these is what's the neural basis of
consciousness?

00:59:20.109 --> 00:59:21.640
Another is what is the neural code?

00:59:21.640 --> 00:59:27.450
The kind of neural networks that Yann has
created are rooted in a kind of view of the

00:59:27.450 --> 00:59:29.819
neural code involving changing weights.

00:59:29.819 --> 00:59:33.710
In recent years, something people have thought,
"Okay, that's surely an important part of

00:59:33.710 --> 00:59:36.819
the puzzle, but maybe there's other parts
of the puzzle."

00:59:36.819 --> 00:59:41.750
It's not simply about what's connected with
what at what level of connectivity, and this

00:59:41.750 --> 00:59:47.460
is what underlies connectomics ... Rather
than viewing the brain as a highway system

00:59:47.460 --> 00:59:51.230
of different connections, it's more like a
train track system where there's constant

00:59:51.230 --> 00:59:53.309
sudden switches.

00:59:53.309 --> 00:59:58.170
This piece of track can be part of an epi-connectivity
between Boston and San Diego or an epi-connectivity

00:59:58.170 --> 01:00:02.520
between Boston and San Francisco depending
on these shuts.

01:00:02.520 --> 01:00:07.500
Maybe the neural code is actually a very dynamic
neural code with these rapid synaptic weight

01:00:07.500 --> 01:00:08.500
changes.

01:00:08.500 --> 01:00:10.049
That's one direction.

01:00:10.049 --> 01:00:14.490
More recently, some people have argued, and
if this turns out to be true, it will be revolutionary,

01:00:14.490 --> 01:00:21.460
that memories and information, in general,
is not only stored in synaptic weights but

01:00:21.460 --> 01:00:23.170
actually inside the cell.

01:00:23.170 --> 01:00:30.210
There's some really incredible work done by
Tonegawa at MIT or, I think, David Glanzman

01:00:30.210 --> 01:00:35.490
at UCLA, that I think has convincingly shown
that synaptic weights might be the path to

01:00:35.490 --> 01:00:40.079
accessing the information, but the actual
information might lie inside the cell.

01:00:40.079 --> 01:00:42.000
Glanzman says it's patterns of methylation
on DNA.

01:00:42.000 --> 01:00:43.000
Now, that's really radical.

01:00:43.000 --> 01:00:47.089
He's the only one saying that but if it's
true, it will change everything.

01:00:47.089 --> 01:00:53.119
We have so far to go in understanding the
brain and present AI is based upon a metaphor

01:00:53.119 --> 01:00:59.000
of neural nets as understood in the brain
10, 20 years ago, but it's changing very fast

01:00:59.000 --> 01:01:01.579
in real brain science.

01:01:01.579 --> 01:01:06.539
My guess is once we crack the neural code,
it will be as momentous for our society as

01:01:06.539 --> 01:01:08.050
the cracking of the genetic code.

01:01:08.050 --> 01:01:09.050
All right, Susan.

01:01:09.050 --> 01:01:10.050
Very interesting.

01:01:10.050 --> 01:01:12.542
I want to hear it ... We have a couple minutes
left.

01:01:12.542 --> 01:01:13.930
Susan, how do we make it good in the future?

01:01:13.930 --> 01:01:22.299
Well, we could have an AI that becomes AGI
and then rapidly evolves into superintelligence.

01:01:22.299 --> 01:01:28.240
Whether it be based on the neural code in
the brain or on something highly not brain-like,

01:01:28.240 --> 01:01:30.760
it could very quickly change its own architecture.

01:01:30.760 --> 01:01:35.630
Then, I wonder, how we'll be able to stay
abreast of it.

01:01:35.630 --> 01:01:38.740
We have to hit the ground running on AI safety.

01:01:38.740 --> 01:01:40.460
I wholeheartedly agree with Max.

01:01:40.460 --> 01:01:46.220
I also wanted to add something which has not
been discussed, which is, I think, as a society,

01:01:46.220 --> 01:01:49.030
we need to think about this idea of merging
with AI.

01:01:49.030 --> 01:01:55.690
Elon Musk has recently suggested that in order
for us to keep up with technological unemployment

01:01:55.690 --> 01:02:03.900
and to deal with the threat of super intelligence,
we, ourselves, need to bring AI into the brain.

01:02:03.900 --> 01:02:09.819
I think that as a culture, we need to start
discussing that AI will not be a world that

01:02:09.819 --> 01:02:16.309
looks like the Jetsons where they're unenhanced
humans surrounded by all this fancy robotic

01:02:16.309 --> 01:02:17.309
equipment.

01:02:17.309 --> 01:02:20.190
The AI will change us as well.

01:02:20.190 --> 01:02:22.329
I just want to leave you with that thought.

01:02:22.329 --> 01:02:25.780
I like that thought.

01:02:25.780 --> 01:02:31.000
Thank you.

01:02:31.000 --> 01:02:36.210
Thank you.

